\description{
The \strong{roster / enrollment frame} and the only place students are counted:
grade, EL status, demographics, program fields, outcomes, \code{ELP_FORM} -- and
\strong{no measurement columns}. It exists because the base table only has rows
where a test happened: enrolled-but-untested years, former ELs and never-ELs
live only here.
}
\section{Counting}{

This is the denominator for anything counted per student-year. Never count
students from \link{academic_long_sim} (it double-counts, once per subject) or
from \link{el_long_sim} (it counts a student up to eight times).
}

\section{Weighting}{

Never-EL rows are a 10\% sample carrying \code{SAMPLE_WEIGHT = 10}; ever-EL rows
carry 1. Every statewide share must weight and every within-EL statistic
must not. Unweighted, current ELs are 52.7\% of SY2023 enrollment; weighted,
17.8\%.
}

\section{Structural missingness}{

By design, not a data problem (contract 3.6). Never-EL rows have every
EL-program field \code{NA}. Former rows have no base-table rows -- formers do not
test. Outcome columns are grade-12 only.
}

\section{Exit dating}{

\code{EXIT_OBSERVED} is 1 on the \strong{last current} row, which is the simulator's
convention and \strong{not} the package's. The package dates exit to the first
year observed as \code{former}, so the package's exit year is this row's
\code{YEAR + 1}. The sim's \code{YEAR_IN_STATUS} and \code{EXIT_OBSERVED} are validation
inputs, never the source of the package's own derivations.
}

